{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01T3UuiQxci4mqXcSX3VCtRU","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t\\begin{abstract}\n2\tRecent methods for collision avoidance in Vision-Language-Action (VLA) models report high Collision Avoidance Rate (CAR) on safety benchmarks and claim effective obstacle avoidance. We show that these metrics can be systematically contaminated by action collapse: the model ceases to produce meaningful motion, and the resulting inaction is counted as successful avoidance. On the SafeLIBERO benchmark, a Signed Distance Field (SDF)-augmented VLA reports 91.5\\% CAR alongside 0\\% task success, yet 99.6\\% of its safe episodes exhibit end-effector displacement below 0.1\\,mm. This contamination is not method-specific; a baseline without any collision objective achieves 82\\% CAR with 99.4\\% of safe episodes below the same threshold, and Pi0.5, which achieves 74.7\\% task success, shows the same pattern. We propose a corrective metric, displacement-filtered CAR (CAR$_\\text{f}$), that excludes sub-threshold episodes. Two structural issues compound the problem. First, Task Success Rate (TSR) and CAR are anti-correlated: models that move the robot through the workspace collide, while collapsed models appear safe. Second, ground-truth SDF oracle experiments demonstrate that end-effector-only refinement cannot prevent full-body collisions, as SDF gradients that repel the end-effector simultaneously drive arm links into obstacle volumes. Preliminary results indicate that training-time SDF supervision on a sufficiently capable VLA can produce genuine collision avoidance with zero frozen rate. Our findings establish that displacement filtering is necessary for meaningful VLA safety evaluation.\n3\t\\end{abstract}"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_0182yoWwbFWbBz3h1nGm58Jy","session_id":"82fe1c25-39c0-4259-b5cc-ba16f1a4fb53","uuid":"a72d01ac-779e-4c63-b45f-0d96fe7309a8","timestamp":"2026-05-25T23:20:56.898Z"}
