{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_0182yoWwbFWbBz3h1nGm58Jy","type":"tool_result","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Now I have a comprehensive understanding of the paper. Let me produce the review.\n\n## R2 Review: Novelty & Related Work\n\n### Summary\nThe paper identifies that Collision Avoidance Rate (CAR), the primary safety metric for VLA evaluation, is systematically contaminated by action collapse — models that stop moving are incorrectly counted as safe. The authors propose displacement-filtered CAR (CAR_f) as a corrective metric and provide supporting evidence through oracle SDF experiments showing structural limitations of end-effector-only refinement. The contribution is primarily diagnostic/methodological, with SDF training as a case study demonstrating genuine (but costly) collision avoidance.\n\n### Strengths\n\n1. **Well-identified metric validity gap.** The observation that 99.6% of \"safe\" episodes exhibit sub-threshold displacement is a clean, quantitative demonstration of a real evaluation problem. The decomposition is rigorous and the evidence is internally consistent.\n\n2. **Formalization connects to established literature.** The framing through Goodhart's law and annotation artifacts (NLP analogy) is apt and places the observation within a well-understood conceptual framework of metric validity challenges.\n\n3. **Oracle experiment design is methodologically sound.** Using ground-truth SDF to eliminate prediction error and isolate the structural limitation of EE-only refinement is a clean experimental design that supports a non-trivial negative conclusion.\n\n4. **Related work section (as drafted) is substantially improved over the 3-reference version described in the summary.** The actual LaTeX draft cites approximately 15+ works across three well-organized subsections, which is adequate for a 7-page paper (though gaps remain, see below).\n\n5. **Practical metric contribution with low adoption barrier.** CAR_f requires only end-effector position logging — no new hardware, data collection, or model changes — which makes adoption realistic.\n\n### Weaknesses\n\n1. **The core observation has limited novelty.** That a metric based on collision absence will reward inaction is conceptually straightforward. The robotics/RL community has extensive experience with degenerate solutions to safety constraints (e.g., the \"don't move\" solution to safe RL). The insight is valuable but not surprising to experts in constrained policy optimization. The paper acknowledges this (\"action collapse itself is well recognized\") but claims the interaction with safety metrics is novel — this is a moderate novelty increment, not a paradigm shift.\n\n2. **Displacement-filtered CAR is a minimal fix, not a methodological innovation.** Once the problem is identified, conditioning on motor activity is the obvious solution. The metric contribution amounts to adding a denominator filter — useful, but difficult to argue as a substantial intellectual contribution at a top venue. More sophisticated approaches (e.g., conditioning on task-relevant motion, trajectory quality metrics, or intention-aware evaluation) are not explored.\n\n3. **\"Three failure modes\" taxonomy is underspecified and not clearly novel.** The paper summary mentions action collapse, geometric path safety, and random incompetence as a taxonomy, but this is descriptive labeling of observed behaviors rather than a principled categorization. The paper does not provide formal definitions that would generalize to other settings, nor does it prove these are exhaustive.\n\n4. **The negative SDF finding overlaps substantially with known results.** That end-effector-only constraints are insufficient for full-body collision avoidance is well-established in classical motion planning (whole-body planning literature, configuration-space methods). The paper positions this as novel for the VLA setting, but the structural reason (kinematic chain coupling) is identical to the classical case. The novelty is in demonstrating it empirically for VLAs, which is useful but incremental.\n\n5. **Missing comparison to activity-conditioned metrics in existing work.** The claim that \"none examine whether measured CAR reflects genuine obstacle avoidance\" requires stronger evidence that no prior work has proposed motion-conditioned safety metrics. In RL safety evaluation, distinguishing active safety from passive safety (agent staying in safe states by not acting) has been discussed.\n\n6. **SDF attribution claim is incomplete.** The LoRA-only baseline results are still pending (marked as placeholder in the draft). Without this, the paper cannot determine whether the CAR improvement is from SDF supervision or from LoRA fine-tuning itself — a critical gap for Claim 4. The paper should not be submitted with this unresolved.\n\n7. **Scope remains narrow despite multi-model framing.** The paper evaluates on a single benchmark (SafeLIBERO), a single task suite for most detailed analyses (object_pudding), and two model families from the same lineage (Pi0/Pi0.5). OpenVLA evaluation is mentioned but blocked. The \"cross-model\" claim in the summary (Table 1) conflates different evaluation conditions.\n\n### Questions for Authors\n\n1. Has any prior work on safe RL or constrained policy optimization noted that conservative (low-action) policies trivially satisfy collision-rate metrics? If so, how does your observation differ beyond the VLA-specific context?\n\n2. Why not propose a more informative metric than simple displacement filtering — for example, conditioning on task-relevant progress, trajectory curvature, or comparing to a task-optimal reference trajectory?\n\n3. The Pi0.5 baseline shows 98.2% fake safe rate among safe episodes, yet achieves 74.7% TSR. This means it successfully completes tasks in most episodes but when it is \"safe\" (no collision), it is almost always because it didn't move. Can you clarify: what fraction of Pi0.5's total episodes show action collapse (not just the safe subset)?\n\n4. For the oracle experiments, have you considered full-body SDF oracles (computing SDF for all arm links) as a control to confirm the structural argument? This would distinguish \"EE-only is insufficient\" from \"runtime refinement is insufficient regardless of body coverage.\"\n\n5. The TSR-CAR anti-correlation is presented as a finding, but isn't it trivially explained by the fact that models that attempt tasks will encounter obstacles? What is the deeper insight beyond this mechanical explanation?\n\n### Missing Related Work\n\n**Metric validity and evaluation methodology in robotics/RL:**\n- Amodei et al. (2016), \"Concrete Problems in AI Safety\" — discusses specification gaming and metrics that don't capture intended behavior\n- Krakovna et al. (2020), \"Specification gaming: the flip side of AI ingenuity\" — directly relevant to safety metrics being gamed by degenerate behaviors\n- Thomas et al. (2019), \"Preventing undesirable behavior of intelligent machines\" — discusses Seldonian algorithms and metric validity in safety\n- Dulac-Arnold et al. (2021), \"Challenges of Real-World RL\" — discusses evaluation reliability issues\n\n**Reward hacking / Goodhart's law (cited but underexplored):**\n- Skalse et al. (2022), \"Defining and Characterizing Reward Hacking\" — formal treatment of when metrics diverge from intended objectives\n- Pan et al. (2022), \"The Effects of Reward Misspecification\" — empirical study of reward proxies failing in RL\n\n**Activity-conditioned safety evaluation:**\n- The safe RL literature routinely distinguishes between \"staying safe by not acting\" and \"acting safely\" — e.g., constrained MDP formulations that penalize both constraint violation AND inaction. CPO (Achiam et al., 2017, already cited) implicitly addresses this through reward maximization alongside constraint satisfaction.\n\n**LoRA/PEFT for robotics:**\n- Hu et al. (2022), \"LoRA: Low-Rank Adaptation\" — cited in appendix but not discussed in related work\n- Embodiment-specific LoRA adaptation literature (e.g., works applying LoRA to RT-2, Octo fine-tuning)\n\n**Multi-task learning and catastrophic forgetting (relevant to SDF damage):**\n- The extreme TSR drop with SDF training relates directly to catastrophic forgetting / multi-task interference literature. French (1999), McCloskey & Cohen (1989) for classical references; more recently, auxiliary task interference in multi-task RL (e.g., Hessel et al., 2019 \"Multi-Task Deep RL with PopArt\")\n\n**VLA safety beyond SafeLIBERO:**\n- RoboCasa, BEHAVIOR-1K, or other manipulation benchmarks with safety-relevant scenarios\n- Real-world VLA safety evaluation (e.g., RT-2 deployment safety protocols)\n\n**Mode collapse in diffusion/flow models for robotics:**\n- Beyond Chi et al. (2023) and Dong et al. (2025) already cited, the denoising score matching literature on mode collapse is relevant\n\n### Minor Issues\n\n1. The paper title in main.tex (\"The Safety Illusion...\") differs from the title given in the review context (\"Rethinking VLA Safety Evaluation...\"). Settle on one.\n\n2. Multiple placeholder values remain throughout the draft (marked \\placeholder{}). Tables and figures are incomplete. The paper is clearly not submission-ready.\n\n3. The contribution list uses a non-standard label [C4] for the fourth item, breaking the itemize formatting convention.\n\n4. Pi0.5 is cited without a reference — only \"Pi0\" has a citation (Black et al., 2024). If Pi0.5 is a distinct publication, it needs its own citation.\n\n5. The phrase \"Neither necessary nor sufficient\" (from the paper summary context) overstates the evidence: the paper shows SDF is insufficient alone for good TSR-CAR tradeoff, but does not prove it is unnecessary (since LoRA-only baseline is pending).\n\n6. The related work references two papers (li2026vlasafety, snyder2026beyond) that appear to be 2026 publications — the reviewer cannot verify these exist. If they are fabricated or incorrect, this is a serious integrity issue.\n\n### Scores\n- Originality: 2 (fair — the core observation is useful but not surprising; the metric fix is obvious once stated; the negative SDF result echoes known classical limitations)\n- Clarity: 3 (good — well-written when complete, logical structure, but many placeholders and incomplete results undermine assessment)\n- Significance: 2 (fair — the diagnostic is valuable for the VLA safety sub-community but the contribution is narrow in scope and the fix is minimal)\n- Overall: 5 (marginally below acceptance threshold)\n- Confidence: 4 (high confidence — familiar with safe RL, evaluation methodology, and SDF-based planning literature)\n\n### Key Verdict\nMarginally below acceptance. The paper identifies a real evaluation validity problem and provides clean empirical evidence, but the core observation (inaction inflates safety metrics) has limited novelty for the broader robotics community, the proposed fix (displacement filtering) is technically trivial once the problem is stated, and the incomplete experimental results (pending LoRA baseline, pending OpenVLA, single benchmark) weaken the generalizability claims needed for a top venue. Would benefit from: (1) a more sophisticated metric contribution beyond simple filtering, (2) completed attribution experiments, and (3) evaluation on at least one additional benchmark or model family outside Pi0."},{"type":"text","text":"agentId: ab87c70903e9c12f7 (use SendMessage with to: 'ab87c70903e9c12f7' to continue this agent)\n<usage>total_tokens: 54492\ntool_uses: 19\nduration_ms: 114857</usage>"}]}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"82fe1c25-39c0-4259-b5cc-ba16f1a4fb53","uuid":"1565c1b8-db00-4ba7-ab3b-d962ec19236d","timestamp":"2026-05-25T23:22:29.387Z","tool_use_result":{"status":"completed","prompt":"You are Reviewer R2 for a CoRL-level robotics/ML conference review. Your focus areas are **Novelty/Originality** and **Related Work**. You must produce a thorough, independent review.\n\n## Venue: CoRL (Conference on Robot Learning)\n**Rating Scale (1-10):**\n- 10: Top 5%, seminal paper\n- 9: Top 15%, strong accept\n- 8: Top 50%, clear accept\n- 7: Good paper, accept\n- 6: Marginally above acceptance threshold\n- 5: Marginally below acceptance threshold\n- 4: Ok but not good enough - rejection\n- 3: Clear rejection\n- 2: Strong rejection\n- 1: Trivial or wrong\n\n**Acceptance rate:** ~30%\n\n**Review dimensions:** Originality, Quality/Soundness, Clarity, Significance, Relevance to robot learning, Limitations.\n\n## Paper Summary\n\n**Title:** \"Rethinking VLA Safety Evaluation: When 98% of Collision Avoidance is an Illusion\"\n\n**Core claims:**\n1. CAR (collision avoidance rate) is systematically contaminated in VLA evaluation — three failure modes: action collapse (Pi0), geometric path safety (Pi0.5), random incompetence (OpenVLA).\n2. Displacement-filtered CAR proposed as corrective metric requiring EE displacement > δ=5mm.\n3. LoRA finetuning alone achieves equivalent CAR to LoRA+SDF, making geometric supervision unnecessary.\n4. SDF auxiliary training causes irreversible task damage.\n\n**Key results:**\n\nTable 1 - Cross-model fake safety (object_pudding):\n| Model | TSR | CAR | ΔEE(m) | FSR | Failure Mode |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Pi0 (A1 LoRA+SDF) | 0% | 91.5% | 0.018 | 99.6% | Action collapse |\n| Pi0.5 (zero-shot) | 74.7% | 26.8% | 0.150 | 98.2% | Geom. path safety |\n| OpenVLA-7B (zero-shot) | 2% | 42% | 0.165 | 42% | Random incompetence |\n\nTable 3 - SDF attribution (object_pudding, 30K):\n| Condition | Seeds | TSR | CAR | ΔEE(m) | FSR | ΔCAR vs Base |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Pi0.5 baseline | 3 | 74.7% | 26.8% | 0.150 | 98.2% | — |\n| Pi0.5 + LoRA only | 2 | 35.5% | 50.0% | 0.28 | 35% | +23.2pp |\n| Pi0.5 + LoRA + SDF (λ=0.005) | 3 | 6.8% | 50.5% | 0.50 | 0% | +23.7pp |\n| Pi0.5 + LoRA + SDF (λ=0.01) | 3 | 6.8% | 52.8% | 0.49 | 0% | +26.0pp |\n\nTable 7 - Phase 2 recovery:\n| Stage | TSR | CAR | ΔEE(m) | FSR |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Pi0.5 baseline | 74.7 | 26.8 | 0.150 | 98.2 |\n| After 10K SDF | 9.0 | 55.5 | 0.505 | 0 |\n| After Phase 2 λ=0 | 12.0 | 30.0 | 0.280 | 22 |\n| LoRA-only (ref) | 35.5 | 50.0 | 0.280 | 35 |\n\n**Paper structure:** 7 pages total. Abstract, Intro, Related Work (~0.5 page, 3 paragraphs), Method (Sec 3, displacement-filtered CAR), Experiments (Sec 4, ~3 pages), Discussion (Sec 5, ~1 page), Conclusion, 2 appendices.\n\n**Related Work as written:**\n- VLA Safety Evaluation: cites only SafeLIBERO(2025). Claims CAR adopted as standard without questioning.\n- VLA Models: cites Pi0(2024), OpenVLA(2024). Mentions Pi0.5 without citation.\n- Safety through Geometric Supervision: mentions SDF in classical motion planning and \"recent work\" integrating geometric losses into VLA training — no specific citations.\n\n**References (total 3):**\n- SafeLIBERO(2025)\n- Pi0(2024) (Physical Intelligence)\n- OpenVLA(2024) (Kim et al., ICML 2024)\n\n**Limitations stated:** (1) Only SafeLIBERO; (2) Only 2 model families clearly show fake safety; (3) No full-body SDF or adaptive weighting; (4) LoRA-only uses 2 seeds.\n\n## Review Context\n\nThe paper is framed as an \"evaluation methodology paper\" with SDF as a negative finding case study. The narrative guidance notes: \"SDF 无增量、Phase 2 失败、泛化不足。Claim 4 砍为 negative result appendix。论文 reframe 为 纯 evaluation methodology paper。\" Target acceptance ~40-50%.\n\nThe paper evaluates on SafeLIBERO benchmark only, two scenes (object_pudding, spatial_bowl), three model families (Pi0, Pi0.5, OpenVLA). 6000+ total episodes claimed.\n\n## YOUR TASK (R2: Novelty + Related Work)\n\nEvaluate the following in depth:\n\n### Novelty Assessment\n- How novel is the observation that CAR can be inflated by inaction? Is this surprising to the robotics community?\n- Is the displacement-filtered CAR metric a genuine methodological contribution, or is it an obvious fix once the problem is identified?\n- How novel is the negative finding about SDF? Does the community expect auxiliary geometric losses to work well with VLAs?\n- Is the \"three failure modes\" taxonomy (action collapse, geometric path safety, random incompetence) a real intellectual contribution?\n- Compare to existing work on metric reliability in robotics/RL — has anyone discussed similar issues before?\n\n### Related Work Gaps\n- The related work section has only 3 references total. What critical prior work is missing?\n- Safety metrics critique literature — any prior work on metric gaming/contamination in robotics or RL?\n- SDF for collision avoidance — what specific classical/recent works should be cited?\n- VLA safety beyond SafeLIBERO — what other benchmarks or approaches exist?\n- LoRA/PEFT for robotics — relevant prior work?\n- Reward hacking / Goodhart's law literature — relevant connections?\n- Multi-task learning and catastrophic forgetting (relevant to the SDF damage finding)?\n\n### Positioning and Framing\n- Is the paper correctly positioned as an evaluation methodology paper?\n- Does the framing oversell or undersell the contribution?\n- Is the \"neither necessary nor sufficient\" claim about SDF too strong given the evidence?\n- How does this relate to broader safety evaluation challenges in robotics?\n\n### Impact Assessment\n- Would this paper change how the CoRL community evaluates VLA safety?\n- Is displacement-filtered CAR likely to be adopted?\n- How significant is the negative SDF finding for the field?\n\n## OUTPUT FORMAT\n\nProduce your review in this exact format:\n\n```\n## R2 Review: Novelty & Related Work\n\n### Summary\n[2-3 sentences summarizing the paper's contribution from your perspective]\n\n### Strengths\n[Numbered list, each with a short title and 1-2 sentence elaboration]\n\n### Weaknesses\n[Numbered list, each with a short title and 1-2 sentence elaboration. Be specific about what references are missing, what novelty claims are overstated, etc.]\n\n### Questions for Authors\n[Numbered list of specific questions]\n\n### Missing Related Work\n[Specific papers/areas that should be cited and discussed, organized by topic]\n\n### Minor Issues\n[Presentation, framing, citation format issues]\n\n### Scores\n- Originality: [1-4, where 4=excellent, 3=good, 2=fair, 1=poor]\n- Clarity: [1-4]\n- Significance: [1-4]\n- Overall: [1-10, using the CoRL scale above]\n- Confidence: [1-5]\n\n### Key Verdict\n[1-2 sentences: accept/reject recommendation with primary reason]\n```\n","agentId":"ab87c70903e9c12f7","agentType":"general-purpose","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Now I have a comprehensive understanding of the paper. Let me produce the review.\n\n## R2 Review: Novelty & Related Work\n\n### Summary\nThe paper identifies that Collision Avoidance Rate (CAR), the primary safety metric for VLA evaluation, is systematically contaminated by action collapse — models that stop moving are incorrectly counted as safe. The authors propose displacement-filtered CAR (CAR_f) as a corrective metric and provide supporting evidence through oracle SDF experiments showing structural limitations of end-effector-only refinement. The contribution is primarily diagnostic/methodological, with SDF training as a case study demonstrating genuine (but costly) collision avoidance.\n\n### Strengths\n\n1. **Well-identified metric validity gap.** The observation that 99.6% of \"safe\" episodes exhibit sub-threshold displacement is a clean, quantitative demonstration of a real evaluation problem. The decomposition is rigorous and the evidence is internally consistent.\n\n2. **Formalization connects to established literature.** The framing through Goodhart's law and annotation artifacts (NLP analogy) is apt and places the observation within a well-understood conceptual framework of metric validity challenges.\n\n3. **Oracle experiment design is methodologically sound.** Using ground-truth SDF to eliminate prediction error and isolate the structural limitation of EE-only refinement is a clean experimental design that supports a non-trivial negative conclusion.\n\n4. **Related work section (as drafted) is substantially improved over the 3-reference version described in the summary.** The actual LaTeX draft cites approximately 15+ works across three well-organized subsections, which is adequate for a 7-page paper (though gaps remain, see below).\n\n5. **Practical metric contribution with low adoption barrier.** CAR_f requires only end-effector position logging — no new hardware, data collection, or model changes — which makes adoption realistic.\n\n### Weaknesses\n\n1. **The core observation has limited novelty.** That a metric based on collision absence will reward inaction is conceptually straightforward. The robotics/RL community has extensive experience with degenerate solutions to safety constraints (e.g., the \"don't move\" solution to safe RL). The insight is valuable but not surprising to experts in constrained policy optimization. The paper acknowledges this (\"action collapse itself is well recognized\") but claims the interaction with safety metrics is novel — this is a moderate novelty increment, not a paradigm shift.\n\n2. **Displacement-filtered CAR is a minimal fix, not a methodological innovation.** Once the problem is identified, conditioning on motor activity is the obvious solution. The metric contribution amounts to adding a denominator filter — useful, but difficult to argue as a substantial intellectual contribution at a top venue. More sophisticated approaches (e.g., conditioning on task-relevant motion, trajectory quality metrics, or intention-aware evaluation) are not explored.\n\n3. **\"Three failure modes\" taxonomy is underspecified and not clearly novel.** The paper summary mentions action collapse, geometric path safety, and random incompetence as a taxonomy, but this is descriptive labeling of observed behaviors rather than a principled categorization. The paper does not provide formal definitions that would generalize to other settings, nor does it prove these are exhaustive.\n\n4. **The negative SDF finding overlaps substantially with known results.** That end-effector-only constraints are insufficient for full-body collision avoidance is well-established in classical motion planning (whole-body planning literature, configuration-space methods). The paper positions this as novel for the VLA setting, but the structural reason (kinematic chain coupling) is identical to the classical case. The novelty is in demonstrating it empirically for VLAs, which is useful but incremental.\n\n5. **Missing comparison to activity-conditioned metrics in existing work.** The claim that \"none examine whether measured CAR reflects genuine obstacle avoidance\" requires stronger evidence that no prior work has proposed motion-conditioned safety metrics. In RL safety evaluation, distinguishing active safety from passive safety (agent staying in safe states by not acting) has been discussed.\n\n6. **SDF attribution claim is incomplete.** The LoRA-only baseline results are still pending (marked as placeholder in the draft). Without this, the paper cannot determine whether the CAR improvement is from SDF supervision or from LoRA fine-tuning itself — a critical gap for Claim 4. The paper should not be submitted with this unresolved.\n\n7. **Scope remains narrow despite multi-model framing.** The paper evaluates on a single benchmark (SafeLIBERO), a single task suite for most detailed analyses (object_pudding), and two model families from the same lineage (Pi0/Pi0.5). OpenVLA evaluation is mentioned but blocked. The \"cross-model\" claim in the summary (Table 1) conflates different evaluation conditions.\n\n### Questions for Authors\n\n1. Has any prior work on safe RL or constrained policy optimization noted that conservative (low-action) policies trivially satisfy collision-rate metrics? If so, how does your observation differ beyond the VLA-specific context?\n\n2. Why not propose a more informative metric than simple displacement filtering — for example, conditioning on task-relevant progress, trajectory curvature, or comparing to a task-optimal reference trajectory?\n\n3. The Pi0.5 baseline shows 98.2% fake safe rate among safe episodes, yet achieves 74.7% TSR. This means it successfully completes tasks in most episodes but when it is \"safe\" (no collision), it is almost always because it didn't move. Can you clarify: what fraction of Pi0.5's total episodes show action collapse (not just the safe subset)?\n\n4. For the oracle experiments, have you considered full-body SDF oracles (computing SDF for all arm links) as a control to confirm the structural argument? This would distinguish \"EE-only is insufficient\" from \"runtime refinement is insufficient regardless of body coverage.\"\n\n5. The TSR-CAR anti-correlation is presented as a finding, but isn't it trivially explained by the fact that models that attempt tasks will encounter obstacles? What is the deeper insight beyond this mechanical explanation?\n\n### Missing Related Work\n\n**Metric validity and evaluation methodology in robotics/RL:**\n- Amodei et al. (2016), \"Concrete Problems in AI Safety\" — discusses specification gaming and metrics that don't capture intended behavior\n- Krakovna et al. (2020), \"Specification gaming: the flip side of AI ingenuity\" — directly relevant to safety metrics being gamed by degenerate behaviors\n- Thomas et al. (2019), \"Preventing undesirable behavior of intelligent machines\" — discusses Seldonian algorithms and metric validity in safety\n- Dulac-Arnold et al. (2021), \"Challenges of Real-World RL\" — discusses evaluation reliability issues\n\n**Reward hacking / Goodhart's law (cited but underexplored):**\n- Skalse et al. (2022), \"Defining and Characterizing Reward Hacking\" — formal treatment of when metrics diverge from intended objectives\n- Pan et al. (2022), \"The Effects of Reward Misspecification\" — empirical study of reward proxies failing in RL\n\n**Activity-conditioned safety evaluation:**\n- The safe RL literature routinely distinguishes between \"staying safe by not acting\" and \"acting safely\" — e.g., constrained MDP formulations that penalize both constraint violation AND inaction. CPO (Achiam et al., 2017, already cited) implicitly addresses this through reward maximization alongside constraint satisfaction.\n\n**LoRA/PEFT for robotics:**\n- Hu et al. (2022), \"LoRA: Low-Rank Adaptation\" — cited in appendix but not discussed in related work\n- Embodiment-specific LoRA adaptation literature (e.g., works applying LoRA to RT-2, Octo fine-tuning)\n\n**Multi-task learning and catastrophic forgetting (relevant to SDF damage):**\n- The extreme TSR drop with SDF training relates directly to catastrophic forgetting / multi-task interference literature. French (1999), McCloskey & Cohen (1989) for classical references; more recently, auxiliary task interference in multi-task RL (e.g., Hessel et al., 2019 \"Multi-Task Deep RL with PopArt\")\n\n**VLA safety beyond SafeLIBERO:**\n- RoboCasa, BEHAVIOR-1K, or other manipulation benchmarks with safety-relevant scenarios\n- Real-world VLA safety evaluation (e.g., RT-2 deployment safety protocols)\n\n**Mode collapse in diffusion/flow models for robotics:**\n- Beyond Chi et al. (2023) and Dong et al. (2025) already cited, the denoising score matching literature on mode collapse is relevant\n\n### Minor Issues\n\n1. The paper title in main.tex (\"The Safety Illusion...\") differs from the title given in the review context (\"Rethinking VLA Safety Evaluation...\"). Settle on one.\n\n2. Multiple placeholder values remain throughout the draft (marked \\placeholder{}). Tables and figures are incomplete. The paper is clearly not submission-ready.\n\n3. The contribution list uses a non-standard label [C4] for the fourth item, breaking the itemize formatting convention.\n\n4. Pi0.5 is cited without a reference — only \"Pi0\" has a citation (Black et al., 2024). If Pi0.5 is a distinct publication, it needs its own citation.\n\n5. The phrase \"Neither necessary nor sufficient\" (from the paper summary context) overstates the evidence: the paper shows SDF is insufficient alone for good TSR-CAR tradeoff, but does not prove it is unnecessary (since LoRA-only baseline is pending).\n\n6. The related work references two papers (li2026vlasafety, snyder2026beyond) that appear to be 2026 publications — the reviewer cannot verify these exist. If they are fabricated or incorrect, this is a serious integrity issue.\n\n### Scores\n- Originality: 2 (fair — the core observation is useful but not surprising; the metric fix is obvious once stated; the negative SDF result echoes known classical limitations)\n- Clarity: 3 (good — well-written when complete, logical structure, but many placeholders and incomplete results undermine assessment)\n- Significance: 2 (fair — the diagnostic is valuable for the VLA safety sub-community but the contribution is narrow in scope and the fix is minimal)\n- Overall: 5 (marginally below acceptance threshold)\n- Confidence: 4 (high confidence — familiar with safe RL, evaluation methodology, and SDF-based planning literature)\n\n### Key Verdict\nMarginally below acceptance. The paper identifies a real evaluation validity problem and provides clean empirical evidence, but the core observation (inaction inflates safety metrics) has limited novelty for the broader robotics community, the proposed fix (displacement filtering) is technically trivial once the problem is stated, and the incomplete experimental results (pending LoRA baseline, pending OpenVLA, single benchmark) weaken the generalizability claims needed for a top venue. Would benefit from: (1) a more sophisticated metric contribution beyond simple filtering, (2) completed attribution experiments, and (3) evaluation on at least one additional benchmark or model family outside Pi0."}],"totalDurationMs":114857,"totalTokens":54492,"totalToolUseCount":19,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2285,"cache_read_input_tokens":49653,"output_tokens":2553,"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":2285},"inference_geo":"","iterations":[{"input_tokens":1,"output_tokens":2553,"cache_read_input_tokens":49653,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2285,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":2285,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard"},"toolStats":{"readCount":11,"searchCount":3,"bashCount":0,"editFileCount":0,"linesAdded":0,"linesRemoved":0,"otherToolCount":5}}}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_019qRhuLaN2cPy4esXhn1J1C","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Both reviews are done. Now I need to run the roundtable process:\n- Step 1: Independent reviews (done - R1 and R2 have produced their reviews)\n- Step 2: Cross-sharing (each reviewer reads the other's review)\n- Step 3: Convergence (discussion to reach consensus)\n- Step 4: Synthesis (final combined report)\n\nLet me now do Step 2: Cross-sharing. I'll send each reviewer the other's review and ask them to respond. But actually, I'll do this as a roundtable discussion myself, since I have both reviews in context.\n\nLet me proceed to Step 2 and Step 3 as a combined synthesis agent that sees both reviews and produces the final roundtable discussion + synthesized report.\n\n\nNow I'll synthesize both reviews by identifying where they align and where they diverge, then produce a converged assessment and final report that I can submit.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":10155,"cache_read_input_tokens":54888,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":10155},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"82fe1c25-39c0-4259-b5cc-ba16f1a4fb53","uuid":"e22d664a-7393-4071-a182-432f9d967d8f"}
