acement-filtered CAR as a corrective. The contribution is primarily diagnostic/methodological with SDF training as a case study.\n\n### Strengths\n1. **Well-identified metric validity gap.** Clean, quantitative demonstration of a real evaluation problem.\n2. **Formalization connects to established literature** (Goodhart's law, annotation artifacts).\n3. **Practical metric contribution with low adoption barrier.** Only needs EE position logging.\n\n### Weaknesses\n1. **The core observation has limited novelty.** That a metric based on collision absence rewards inaction is conceptually straightforward. Safe RL has extensive experience with \"don't move\" solutions.\n2. **Displacement-filtered CAR is a minimal fix, not a methodological innovation.** Once the problem is identified, conditioning on motor activity is the obvious solution.\n3. **\"Three failure modes\" taxonomy is underspecified and not clearly novel.** Descriptive labeling rather than a principled categorization.\n4. **The negative SDF finding overlaps with known classical results.** EE-only constraints being insufficient is well-established in motion planning.\n5. **Missing comparison to activity-conditioned metrics in existing work.**\n6. **SDF attribution claim is incomplete** (LoRA-only baseline pending per draft review).\n7. **Scope remains narrow** — single benchmark, two scenes, related model families.\n\n### Missing Related Work\n- Specification gaming literature (Krakovna et al., 2020; Amodei et al., 2016)\n- Reward hacking (Skalse et al., 2022; Pan et al., 2022)\n- Catastrophic forgetting for SDF damage finding\n- LoRA/PEFT for robotics\n- VLA safety beyond SafeLIBERO\n- Activity-conditioned safety in safe RL\n\n### Scores\n- Originality: 2 (fair)\n- Clarity: 3 (good)\n- Significance: 2 (fair)\n- Overall: 5 (marginally below acceptance threshold)\n- Confidence: 4\n\n### Key Verdict\nMarginally below acceptance. Real evaluation problem identified, but limited novelty (inaction inflates safety metrics is not surprising), trivial fix, incomplete experiments, narrow scope.\n\n---\n\n## YOUR TASK: Roundtable Synthesis\n\n### Step 2: Cross-Sharing Analysis\nIdentify:\n- Points of agreement between R1 and R2\n- Points of disagreement\n- Complementary insights that strengthen the overall assessment\n\n### Step 3: Convergence\nFor each disagreement:\n- Which reviewer's position is better supported?\n- What is the consensus view?\n\n### Step 4: Final Synthesis\nProduce the final unified review report in this exact format:\n\n```\n# Roundtable Review Report\n\n## Cross-Sharing Summary\n[Key agreements and disagreements between reviewers]\n\n## Consensus Assessment\n\n### Paper Summary\n[3-4 sentences]\n\n### Strengths (Consensus)\n[Numbered, merged from both reviewers, deduplicated]\n\n### Weaknesses (Consensus)\n[Numbered, merged, with severity ratings: Critical/Major/Minor]\n\n### Required Revisions (for potential acceptance)\n[Ordered by priority]\n\n### Questions for Authors\n[Merged, deduplicated, ordered by importance]\n\n### Missing Related Work\n[From R2, validated by roundtable]\n\n## Final Scores\n\n| Dimension | R1 | R2 | Consensus |\n|-----------|----|----|-----------|\n| Soundness/Quality | X/4 | — | X/4 |\n| Originality | — | X/4 | X/4 |\n| Clarity | X/4 | X/4 | X/4 |\n| Significance | X/4 | X/4 | X/4 |\n| Overall | X/10 | X/10 | X/10 |\n| Confidence | X/5 | X/5 | X/5 |\n\n## Meta-Review\n[3-5 sentences: overall recommendation, primary reasons, what would change the decision]\n```\n","uuid":"3df17ac9-dbcd-466b-831c-6343677e73e3","session_id":"82fe1c25-39c0-4259-b5cc-ba16f1a4fb53"}
