{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01DyoKo4FqNkD3acCqEXQcfj","type":"tool_result","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Roundtable synthesis complete. The unified review report has been submitted.\n\n**Key findings:**\n\n- **Consensus score: 5/10 (marginally below acceptance)** with confidence 4/5\n- Both reviewers agree on the overall verdict independently\n- The significance disagreement (R1=3 vs R2=2) resolves toward R2's more conservative position — the core observation has limited novelty beyond the VLA-specific community\n- **Three blockers to acceptance:** (1) statistical insufficiency for the LoRA-only claim (2 seeds, no CIs), (2) no δ threshold sensitivity analysis, (3) missing positioning against specification gaming literature\n- The paper's path to acceptance is clear: third seed + stats, threshold sweep, broader framing, and ideally a second benchmark\n\nReport saved to: `/home/ubuntu/.agent-ml-research-be-idea-gen-0515/projects/diff_collision_sdf/artifacts/dream_reports/dream_roundtable_corl/best/review_report.md`"},{"type":"text","text":"agentId: a652c8bf71f049815 (use SendMessage with to: 'a652c8bf71f049815' to continue this agent)\n<usage>total_tokens: 22425\ntool_uses: 3\nduration_ms: 78471</usage>"}]}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"82fe1c25-39c0-4259-b5cc-ba16f1a4fb53","uuid":"603481a4-e2dd-45c2-9a82-8a216de28978","timestamp":"2026-05-25T23:24:43.049Z","tool_use_result":{"status":"completed","prompt":"You are the Roundtable Chair for a CoRL paper review. You have received independent reviews from R1 (Methods+Experiments) and R2 (Novelty+Related Work). Your task is to:\n1. **Cross-share**: Identify where the reviewers agree and disagree\n2. **Converge**: Resolve disagreements and reach consensus\n3. **Synthesize**: Produce a final unified review report\n\n## Paper\n**Title:** \"Rethinking VLA Safety Evaluation: When 98% of Collision Avoidance is an Illusion\"\n\nThe paper identifies that CAR (collision avoidance rate) is systematically contaminated in VLA safety evaluation (three failure modes: action collapse, geometric path safety, random incompetence), proposes displacement-filtered CAR as a corrective metric, and provides a negative finding that SDF auxiliary training is neither necessary (LoRA-only suffices) nor sufficient (irreversible task damage) for VLA collision avoidance.\n\n## R1 Review (Methods + Experiments)\n\n### Summary\nThis paper identifies systematic contamination of the Collision Avoidance Rate (CAR) metric in VLA safety evaluation, proposes displacement-filtered CAR as a corrective metric, and uses SDF auxiliary training as a diagnostic tool to expose the metric's failure modes. The core contribution is methodological: demonstrating that 98%+ of apparent safety on SafeLIBERO is illusory, arising from action collapse, geometric path safety, or random incompetence rather than genuine avoidance behavior.\n\n### Strengths\n1. **Well-motivated metric critique with clear mechanistic taxonomy.** The decomposition of fake safety into three distinct failure modes (action collapse in Pi0, geometric path safety in Pi0.5, random incompetence in OpenVLA) is clean and provides practitioners with actionable diagnostic categories.\n2. **Displacement-filtered CAR is simple and implementable.** Straightforward to compute from existing evaluation infrastructure.\n3. **Large experimental effort with iterative refinement.** 70+ experiments and 6000+ episodes, including careful debugging.\n4. **Phase 2 irreversibility finding is scientifically interesting.** SDF causes permanent representational damage.\n5. **Honest negative results framing.** Does not oversell SDF as a solution.\n\n### Weaknesses\n1. **Critical statistical gap: LoRA-only uses 2 seeds vs. 3 for SDF; \"statistically indistinguishable\" claim is unjustified.** The SDF delta over LoRA-only is +2.5pp. With only 2 seeds and 200 episodes per seed, confidence interval is ±5-7pp. No formal hypothesis test is provided.\n2. **No sensitivity analysis for δ=5mm threshold.** No justification for this specific value.\n3. **Cross-model comparison (Table 1) compares fundamentally different experimental conditions.** Pi0 is finetuned, others are zero-shot.\n4. **Phase 2 irreversibility claim based on single seed.** Different seeds or hyperparameters might yield different recovery.\n5. **spatial_bowl +9pp with 200 episodes — significance unclear.** No LoRA-only control on spatial_bowl.\n6. **OpenVLA: 15% NaN episodes create selection bias.** Should report CAR including/excluding NaN episodes.\n7. **FSR metric is redundant.** Contains no information beyond filtered CAR and raw CAR jointly.\n\n### Scores\n- Soundness: 2 (fair)\n- Clarity: 3 (good)\n- Significance: 3 (good)\n- Overall: 5 (marginally below acceptance threshold)\n- Confidence: 4\n\n### Key Verdict\nMarginally below acceptance. The diagnostic contribution is valuable but the central experimental claim rests on insufficient statistical evidence (2 seeds, no CIs). Third LoRA seed + threshold sensitivity + Phase 2 strengthening would likely push to acceptance.\n\n---\n\n## R2 Review (Novelty + Related Work)\n\n### Summary\nThe paper identifies that CAR is systematically contaminated by action collapse and proposes displacement-filtered CAR as a corrective. The contribution is primarily diagnostic/methodological with SDF training as a case study.\n\n### Strengths\n1. **Well-identified metric validity gap.** Clean, quantitative demonstration of a real evaluation problem.\n2. **Formalization connects to established literature** (Goodhart's law, annotation artifacts).\n3. **Practical metric contribution with low adoption barrier.** Only needs EE position logging.\n\n### Weaknesses\n1. **The core observation has limited novelty.** That a metric based on collision absence rewards inaction is conceptually straightforward. Safe RL has extensive experience with \"don't move\" solutions.\n2. **Displacement-filtered CAR is a minimal fix, not a methodological innovation.** Once the problem is identified, conditioning on motor activity is the obvious solution.\n3. **\"Three failure modes\" taxonomy is underspecified and not clearly novel.** Descriptive labeling rather than a principled categorization.\n4. **The negative SDF finding overlaps with known classical results.** EE-only constraints being insufficient is well-established in motion planning.\n5. **Missing comparison to activity-conditioned metrics in existing work.**\n6. **SDF attribution claim is incomplete** (LoRA-only baseline pending per draft review).\n7. **Scope remains narrow** — single benchmark, two scenes, related model families.\n\n### Missing Related Work\n- Specification gaming literature (Krakovna et al., 2020; Amodei et al., 2016)\n- Reward hacking (Skalse et al., 2022; Pan et al., 2022)\n- Catastrophic forgetting for SDF damage finding\n- LoRA/PEFT for robotics\n- VLA safety beyond SafeLIBERO\n- Activity-conditioned safety in safe RL\n\n### Scores\n- Originality: 2 (fair)\n- Clarity: 3 (good)\n- Significance: 2 (fair)\n- Overall: 5 (marginally below acceptance threshold)\n- Confidence: 4\n\n### Key Verdict\nMarginally below acceptance. Real evaluation problem identified, but limited novelty (inaction inflates safety metrics is not surprising), trivial fix, incomplete experiments, narrow scope.\n\n---\n\n## YOUR TASK: Roundtable Synthesis\n\n### Step 2: Cross-Sharing Analysis\nIdentify:\n- Points of agreement between R1 and R2\n- Points of disagreement\n- Complementary insights that strengthen the overall assessment\n\n### Step 3: Convergence\nFor each disagreement:\n- Which reviewer's position is better supported?\n- What is the consensus view?\n\n### Step 4: Final Synthesis\nProduce the final unified review report in this exact format:\n\n```\n# Roundtable Review Report\n\n## Cross-Sharing Summary\n[Key agreements and disagreements between reviewers]\n\n## Consensus Assessment\n\n### Paper Summary\n[3-4 sentences]\n\n### Strengths (Consensus)\n[Numbered, merged from both reviewers, deduplicated]\n\n### Weaknesses (Consensus)\n[Numbered, merged, with severity ratings: Critical/Major/Minor]\n\n### Required Revisions (for potential acceptance)\n[Ordered by priority]\n\n### Questions for Authors\n[Merged, deduplicated, ordered by importance]\n\n### Missing Related Work\n[From R2, validated by roundtable]\n\n## Final Scores\n\n| Dimension | R1 | R2 | Consensus |\n|-----------|----|----|-----------|\n| Soundness/Quality | X/4 | — | X/4 |\n| Originality | — | X/4 | X/4 |\n| Clarity | X/4 | X/4 | X/4 |\n| Significance | X/4 | X/4 | X/4 |\n| Overall | X/10 | X/10 | X/10 |\n| Confidence | X/5 | X/5 | X/5 |\n\n## Meta-Review\n[3-5 sentences: overall recommendation, primary reasons, what would change the decision]\n```\n","agentId":"a652c8bf71f049815","agentType":"general-purpose","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Roundtable synthesis complete. The unified review report has been submitted.\n\n**Key findings:**\n\n- **Consensus score: 5/10 (marginally below acceptance)** with confidence 4/5\n- Both reviewers agree on the overall verdict independently\n- The significance disagreement (R1=3 vs R2=2) resolves toward R2's more conservative position — the core observation has limited novelty beyond the VLA-specific community\n- **Three blockers to acceptance:** (1) statistical insufficiency for the LoRA-only claim (2 seeds, no CIs), (2) no δ threshold sensitivity analysis, (3) missing positioning against specification gaming literature\n- The paper's path to acceptance is clear: third seed + stats, threshold sweep, broader framing, and ideally a second benchmark\n\nReport saved to: `/home/ubuntu/.agent-ml-research-be-idea-gen-0515/projects/diff_collision_sdf/artifacts/dream_reports/dream_roundtable_corl/best/review_report.md`"}],"totalDurationMs":78471,"totalTokens":22425,"totalToolUseCount":3,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2542,"cache_read_input_tokens":19644,"output_tok